  "alloy_systems": [
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_1",
      "name": "Ni-based superalloy",
      "material_category": "superalloy",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "1",
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "Two outstanding aerospace-oriented high-temperature materials, the single-crystal nickel-based superalloys for high-pressure turbine blades and the γ-TiAl-based alloys for low pressure turbine blades, are being presented here.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "25c15f25d57a2aa0:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": "7",
          "section": "2.4 Interactions with protective coatings",
          "quote": "A major issue for coated SC nickel-based superalloys is the chemical interactions between the superalloy substrate and the coating, occurring during the coating process and, later, in service at high temperatures.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "25c15f25d57a2aa0:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_2",
      "name": "γ-TiAl alloy",
      "material_category": "intermetallic alloy",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "2",
          "section": "1. General introduction",
          "quote": "Substitute lighter materials have been sought for, especially in the low-pressure turbine (LPT), where the conditions are not as extreme as in the HP turbine, one example being the γ-TiAl alloys.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "25c15f25d57a2aa0:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": "9",
          "section": "3.1 Introduction",
          "quote": "In this context, TiAl alloys, exhibiting a density twice lower (∼4g ·cm−3) than that of nickel-based superalloys (∼8g ·cm−3), are considered to be really attractive for high-temperature applications owing to their high specific modulus and mechanical strength and to a very good oxidation resistance (Fig. 10) [32,33].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "Fig. 10",
          "chunk_id": "25c15f25d57a2aa0:part:002"
        },
        {
          "page": "13",
          "section": "3.4",
          "quote": "Concerning γ-TiAl alloys, a better consistency in mechanical properties was successfully achieved by using pre-alloyed powder metallurgy (PM).",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "25c15f25d57a2aa0:part:003"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
